    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "If normal faults grow in an Andersonian state of stress, opening mode joints are predicted to be oriented parallel to fault strike. A field example of normal faults and joints is described for which the joint trend is almost perpendicular to the fault strike. The implication is that the state of stress associated with normal fault initiation may not necessarily correlate with the stress state that produced the joints near to the fault. It is more likely that the joints form due to a local stress state around the fault tip. Fault evolution plays an important role in resolution of the near-fault stress field, and thus the orientation of extensional features around the fault, such as joints. Fault-normal joints affect fluid migration direction; compared to joints parallel to fault strike, there is a 90* rotation of the permeability ellipsoid major axis from fault-parallel to fault-orthogonal.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
